2.27.m:
Āyuṣya, Svastyayana, the entire Varmagaṇa, / Vāstoṣpatya, and Raudra—these are declared to be the ten groups.
An oblation of these is to be understood in all rites of pacification, O Bhārgava. / The first three rituals are proclaimed according to distinct principal categories.
For each group, he should give a cow; a cow for the interior deposit, and a cow for the amulet. / A ruler of the earth, however, should give ten for each.
Free from desire, he should also give land to the royal priest. / For Abhayā, an amulet of Varuṇa's [tree] should be made, O foremost of the Bhṛgus;
an amulet of hundred-jointed grass for Amṛtā; and for Saumyā, an amulet made from a conch. / For Vaiṣṇavī, an amulet of trivṛt is declared; for Raudrī, a washed amulet;
for Brāhmī, an amulet of brahmarakṣas; for Maitrī, it should be of gold. / For Vāyavyā, an amulet of aṅgiras is declared, and Vāyavya should be made of conch.
For Kauberī, of udumbara; for Bhārgavī, of asparagus; / for Prājāpatyā, a bilva amulet; for Tvāṣṭrī, a consecrated amulet;

It comes to fruition in the king's body, in the populace, in his city, wives, domestic priest, / in his sons, and likewise in his treasury and army.
Those portents which occur naturally at their assigned times / are to be known as bringing good fortune; hear about them as I speak.
Lightning, thunderbolts, earthquakes, twilight glows, sounds of windless air, / halos, dust, smoke, and a red sun at sunset and sunrise;
the blessing of trees here, flowing with abundant flowers and fruits, / and an increase in cattle, birds, and honey—these are auspicious in the spring months of Madhu and Mādhava.
A solar disk turbid with the falling of stars and meteors, and tawny, / bringing wind amidst fireless blazes, bursts, smoke, and dust;
the twilight red as a red lotus, the sky resembling an agitated ocean, / and the drying up of the water of rivers—having seen [these] in summer, one should declare it auspicious.
Indra's bow, halos, the moon, dry sprouts shooting up, / shaking, overflowing, deformity, and a dread trembling of the earth;
the swelling of rivers, wells, and lakes, the swimming of mules, / and the crumbling of houses by day—these will occur in the rainy season [as auspicious].

2.29.m:
And worship of the denizens of heaven should indeed be performed with the sounds of mṛdaṅgas, drums, and with songs; / likewise, sacrifice to Dhātṛ should be performed according to rule, and food with many cows should be given to twice-borns.
2.142 / Garga said: / If unyoked conveyances move, or yoked ones do not move / when urged, great misfortune would be impending for him.
Musical instruments being played do not sound, or they sound unstruck; / immovable things move, and movable things do not move;
and sounds of trumpets in the sky, or sounds of celestial musicians moving [there]; / if wood turns into an axe or a transformation of a wooden ladle;
if the tail of cows entangles and fells a woman; / or eclipses caused by celestial portents—one should predict dreadful peril from weapons.
Having performed the worship of Vāyu with barley groats, O twice-born, one should recite the mantras prescribed for him; / one should give abundant supreme food here, ending with circumambulation, so that his pacification may occur.

And cows accompanied by gold, a pair of cloths, and upper garments should be given to twice-borns according to rule; / when this is done, the evil that was heralded by wild beasts or birds goes to pacification.
2.144 / Garga said: / The causeless collapse of firm temples, arched gateways, watchtowers, / gates, walls, and dwellings leads to the death of the king.
Where the quarters of the sky are covered with dust or smoke,
and the sun, moon, and stars are discoloured, it leads to an increase of fear.
Where rākṣasas are seen, and brahmins act contrary to dharma,
the seasons are reversed, and the unworthy of worship is worshipped by people;
the asterisms are disjoined—that is a token of great danger;
and the rising of a comet, eclipses, and the piercing of the sun and moon;
where there is an aberration of the planets and asterisms, there too one should predict fear;
and women quarrel, and children strike children;
and the twice-born are bereft of character and proper conduct, fond of liquor, meat, and untruth,
as well as excessively rigid as stone, fallen from the path of the three Vedas;

in whatever country [such things] arise, there one should recognise calamity;
where [gods] are not continually worshipped with the proper sacrificial posts, food, and oblations;
where there occurs an interruption of proper ritual acts;
where the fire does not blaze up when appeasing oblations are poured;
ants and carnivorous [creatures] go to the afflicted person;
full jars leak, or the sacrificial offering is stolen;
where auspicious voices are not heard on every side;
a sneezing person runs, and [people] laugh loudly and weep;
and they do not conduct themselves properly toward the gods and toward brahmins;
and secret faults are voiced, and discordant [instruments] are played;
where people harm their preceptors and friends, and are intent on honouring enemies;
where the populace dishonours brahmins, friends, and ministers;
where atheism arises concerning rites of appeasement, auspicious rites, and oblations into the fire;
there the king dies, or that realm is destroyed.
When the destruction of a king is imminent, hear from me the portents:
first he hates brahmins, and comes into conflict with brahmins;

he is also reviled by brahmins, and seeks to kill brahmins;
he does not remember them in duties, and being petitioned, he is overcome;
he takes delight [in wickedness], is merciless, and does not welcome their praise;
and through greed he imposes unprecedented taxes on the people.

Puṣkara said:
One's own circle [of kings] indeed should be the first circle here;
around it are to be understood the enemies of the circle.
Hear the arrangement regarding the target of attack there as well:
the enemy is deemed the target of attack, and the expectant ally there [is also to be known];
beyond him is to be known the friend, the ally, and the ally's enemy;
this has been told in the front; learn from me also regarding what lies to the rear:
the rear-enemy is behind him, then the rear-ally is spoken of;
another beyond that is the rear-enemy's ally, then the rear-ally's ally is spoken of.
Capable of punishing or favouring an ambitious king who has taken up arms
or laid down arms, O twice-born, is proclaimed the middle king;
he who is capable of punishing or favouring all [others]
is called the neutral king, a mighty ruler of the earth.
Just this circle of twelve kings has been declared to you, O Rāma;
even here no fixed rule can be stated, O bull among men.

There is truly no enemy by birth, nor is there any natural friend;
friends and enemies are produced through the connection of utility.
Enemies are declared to be of three kinds: born of the same family, immediate neighbours, and made;
among these, each preceding one is considered weightier and hardest to remedy.
Even an immediate neighbour who is an enemy is also considered by me as an artificial [enemy];
a king may become a rear-enemy to an enemy who attacks a friend.
One should pacify one's own rear-enemy through diplomatic expedients;
the ancients do not praise the destruction of an enemy by means of an ally.
For an ally becomes an enemy immediately on becoming a neighbour;
the ambitious king should uproot the enemy himself if he is able.

The ambitious king who conquers righteously should thus bring the world into subjugation;
people are terrified of him who conquers unrighteously;
even after obtaining the whole earth, he does not enjoy prosperity for long.

2.30.m:
Rāma said:
Conciliation and division have been described, and likewise gift and force;
force has been described within one's own land; describe [its application] in a foreign land to me.

Entering into an agreement is considered peace, while injury is war;
the marching of one desirous of conquest into the enemy's realm is prescribed as an expedition.
Remaining in one's own country even during hostilities is called encamping;
advancing with half of one's forces is called duplicity.
Seeking refuge with a neutral or intermediary power is known as alliance;
peace should be sought with an equal, and by an inferior with one more powerful.
War should be made personally by a stronger king against an inferior;
even then, a rear-enemy who is stronger should not be allied with.
When an enemy, holding back, is capable of interrupting undertakings,
a powerful king whose rear is insecure should resort to duplicity.
A king who considers himself checked by a powerful foe
should seek refuge; this is the lowest of the policy measures.
Marching is declared to involve great loss, expense, and fatigue for them;
only if it yields great advantage should it be undertaken, O Rāma.

2.32.m:
You are the highest earth together with what roams the mountains;
likewise the sky together with the sun and darkness;
together with the ocean, together with the timiṅgila fish, together with waves, having many sea-monsters, thronged with fish.

You are constantly worshipped with sacrifices here by brahmins for the sake of fruit;
O host of forces, you are sung in the eight distinctions of purpose;
for your sake, the foremost of the twice-born, devoted to sacrifice, master the auxiliary limbs of the Veda together with all the Vedas.
